t {:answer/id #uuid "b54cebe4-6aa6-502d-9b98-b267d3c2bb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380708} :answer-field/id #uuid "08090d20-3972-5c5d-a3b3-2558a12e085f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59a69c0f-c2af-5b88-9b38-1fbc42cfdc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122756" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(-2,-1),$ and $(4,-4).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\,j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-3))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (1)}{2})\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-2,-1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-2))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2})\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,-4) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (4))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-4)}{2})\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f429c0-4106-53ea-9c7e-5a3e5c69557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} {:answer/id #uuid "0560e91f-841f-5133-b19f-414819f58310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}(2{e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362985} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c2c04d-6401-5b56-9894-aeead7dbe986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362986} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc5fcaa-258a-5aeb-90ce-1bd2af6582b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362987} {:answer/id #uuid "a210ea76-975d-59a6-8399-df6d3b035510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f429c0-4106-53ea-9c7e-5a3e5c69557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b62b53e-864d-5c41-bf41-a59840c727e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2b06f7c-408a-5dfe-b281-0fde1f80e146" :question/math-academy-id "q-123354" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d33f9b851125443ff738fa4c3aedcbeaf8a202d05caa06747e74262c0ae89bd9.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dd37ec-93a9-5b21-89e4-561d7cf50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8e4a81-ce37-5a0a-bf71-1d9989cbe036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256881} {:answer/id #uuid "297e7d94-4a80-565f-88bd-e5215df2ba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257151} {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ca18d-520e-5091-a63b-d25c9509aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b3878a-36da-5c94-8d4e-f1633afbca83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c8bb8d5-c684-5d53-8ebb-fc2e85b6f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123355" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa492a8-e07a-522a-bf45-34af18c7a267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{2} θ\\tan^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168598} {:answer/id #uuid "1813a2d0-d5bd-52b4-99b5-efcb1d2a7640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168599} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba48aa-c769-5c6a-9e07-f0c67be25a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + 9\\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168600} {:answer/id #uuid "55f60188-6938-5f92-aae8-dedef3328cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{9 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a3ab0-69fc-59cc-bc73-08e310482454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168602}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4363fdf-f405-59b8-b8f3-f8bb7ba8806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab8694-5f19-5ea3-8d98-092aec26cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405025} {:answer/id #uuid "a57cacb1-b76d-579c-8c67-b4527f5232ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{9+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405026} {:answer/id #uuid "1eabb9a5-ecc0-5827-9b8a-fa684cf58678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405027} {:answer/id #uuid "9f675dbc-4798-5a7f-b170-06e1c7c60f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9492d74f-ea7c-56c6-a9a7-679ef961d276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is obtained by using the pointwise polar arc-length integrand for ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2<\\theta<\\pi /2$? Interpret the endpoint integrals as improper integrals." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese endpoint integrals are improper and diverge to $+\\infty.$ The requested expression is determined by the pointwise arc-length integrand on $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477217b2-25da-5eb5-bf11-b8dba3a034f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257407} {:answer/id #uuid "47c32564-aebd-58a5-91d4-f60e41bdce79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257410} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2954a9-a57d-5b93-b489-ad2d5cb06b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258004} {:answer/id #uuid "d940cf3f-dda0-5356-bac1-c5452d47f35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258684} {:answer/id #uuid "ed844e7d-d658-557e-a456-73ff91011d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b2954a9-a57d-5b93-b489-ad2d5cb06b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258004} :answer-field/id #uuid "343e4e11-bb27-5f39-9989-a4e9e01a1124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "474b9c27-587d-5510-bc2c-fbc91f7d6903" :question/math-academy-id "q-123623" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4c9cc4f-a3ca-5b22-a9a9-481f2fe6587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Exp(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170354} {:answer/id #uuid "19e0b67a-24a4-5072-bc52-060f2a5fd744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Exp(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170355} {:answer/id #uuid "440685a2-ae29-51f9-8634-84a784c93861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Exp(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170356} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc6ca66-e120-5fa2-af91-108a0e973a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Γ(3, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170357} {:answer/id #uuid "527d25dd-2812-5217-95d6-3f3360984385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Γ(5, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170358}] :answer-field/id #uuid "780b21cd-97a0-52a4-bf45-d7da397d0e62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170353}] :question/id #uuid "c303e8fd-873c-5da3-9aad-43cbd74c4762" :question/math-academy-id "q-240417" :question/problem "Consider the independent random variables $Y_{1}∼Γ(2, 3)$ and $Y_{2}∼Exp(3)$. What is the distribution of $Y = Y_{1} + Y_{2}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a, b]$ | $\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right.$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $N(μ, σ^{2})$ | $\\exp (μt + \\frac{1}{2}σ^{2}t^{2})$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $χ^{2}(k)$ | $(1 - 2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t < \\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $Exp(λ)$ | $\\frac{λ}{λ - t}$ | $t < λ$ |\n| $Γ(α, λ)$ | $(\\frac{λ}{λ - t})^{α}$ | $t < λ$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dc57029-131f-588c-9f91-58ab234e3f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170360} {:answer/id #uuid "d0542e21-35b1-53e0-a6ed-da09799995be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170361} {:answer/id #uuid "2807f281-5fb0-5273-899b-5261be151ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170362} {:answer/id #uuid "45bcc294-2db6-5894-9d63-4f31f990f756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170363} {:answer/id #uuid "61f362e8-9d29-5528-b84b-46263a51f05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170364}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3a7c631e-0cc5-5c10-900d-bf6fa7291a87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170359}] :question/id #uuid "495b3383-98ab-5ca8-bd90-0819e8534e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-24909" :question/problem "What is $\\sin (\\arccos (\\frac{1}{2}))$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f02d2d-c10d-5ca4-b6d9-0139b28173a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13a56b51-6b01-5125-b67e-75c6aab58d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-253892" :question/problem "At a particular helpdesk, customer waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $6$ customers per hour. If a customer has already waited for $6$ minutes, what is the probability that they will need to wait at least $4$ more minutes for their call to be answered? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the customer's waiting time, in minutes. Since the help desk serves $6$ customers per hour on average (or $1$ customer every $10$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ customers per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ $s=6,$ and $t=4.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6+4\\,|\\,X>6) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/10)\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/5} \\\\  & ≈0.670.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d052de8a-4f04-5e04-b975-532c813f9c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170884} {:answer/id #uuid "10ff75c3-daa4-53f3-8882-8cf83b4ba9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170885} {:answer/id #uuid "4b146c59-eb19-5c1a-9ad6-dee14307372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170886} {:answer/id #uuid "77248f77-5b52-5bbb-9490-c54f729b58b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170887} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb24657-c3dd-53ab-a504-11156db31320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170888}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0caaede-533a-56f9-b7b6-2d7ce4c9d280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170883}] :question/id #uuid "2c68225f-88f7-5039-acc8-1a1fd40fb051" :question/math-academy-id "q-256397" :question/problem "Consider the independent random variables $Y_{1}∼N(0, 1)$ and $Y_{2}∼N(0, 1)$. What is the distribution of $Y = Y_{1} + Y_{2}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a, b]$ | $\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right.$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $N(μ, σ^{2})$ | $\\exp (μt + \\frac{1}{2}σ^{2}t^{2})$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $χ^{2}(k)$ | $(1 - 2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t < \\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $Exp(λ)$ | $\\frac{λ}{λ - t}$ | $t < λ$ |\n| $Γ(α, λ)$ | $(\\frac{λ}{λ - t})^{α}$ | $t < λ$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a85bf69e-b5a9-5136-8e3a-5948ed2f7f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171154} {:answer/id #uuid "ab33e8dc-7017-590f-b120-78ed51358a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171155} {:answer/id #uuid "ca35dd7b-fe91-54a7-9bdd-1b656ad84925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171156} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb7ca55-55aa-5ead-acd3-80589437ef39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171157} {:answer/id #uuid "443a8c24-7690-5386-8b40-4663d66d69bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171158}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0f9fd6-b0e5-514a-ae58-3b999a67d3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171153}] :question/id #uuid "b0b77533-0e93-5d99-8691-45a65a18762c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66053" :question/problem "What is $\\arcsin (\\sin (\\frac{5π}{4}))$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d43ed1-db60-5105-8ae1-5b7bc83b0b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171160} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf7967f-7bee-57a4-965f-fd76e2e1ee63" :answer/type {:d